Healthcare Intel Digest
October 5, 2026 =C2=B7 Market data through October 2, 2026
Contents
In the News
1. Aggregate MA stability conceals targeted carrier retrenchment
2. Transparency in Coverage shifts from file production to governed data infr=
astructure
3. GLOBE survives as a mandatory model but loses most of its proposed economi=
c reach
4. OIG expands the Medicare preclusion gap into ownership and pharmacy contro=
ls
Market Snapshot
In the News
Executive View
The final 2027 Medicare Advantage landscape resolves the benefit-retrenchment=
 question but reveals a two-speed market.
National plan availability and average premiums appear stable, while major ca=
rriers are pruning underperforming products and shifting toward HMOs and spec=
ial-needs plans.
Federal price-transparency policy moved from data volume toward data usabilit=
y and accountability.
New requirements reduce redundant files but add utilization context, provider=
-taxonomy logic and senior-official attestations, changing the opportunity fo=
r transparency-data vendors.
CMS finalized international reference pricing for Part B drugs, but at a frac=
tion of the proposed economic scale.
GLOBE remains operationally complex for selected providers while targeting le=
ss than 1% of relevant Original Medicare Part B drug spending.
OIG identified a broader Medicare managed-care screening failure.
Plans paid $72 million for services associated with revoked or otherwise prob=
lematic providers, extending the integrity gap from individual suppliers into=
 ownership, pharmacy and list-management controls.
Overall environment: modestly deteriorated for MA plan continuity and payment=
 integrity; improved for transparency-data quality; broadly unchanged for nea=
r-term provider drug economics.
1. Aggregate MA stability conceals targeted carrier retrenchment
What happened
The September 28 Medicare Advantage landscape resolves the preliminary plan-d=
esign signals discussed earlier in September. CMS projects that the weighted =
average monthly MA premium will decline 16.5%, from $14.37 in 2026 to $12.00 =
in 2027. The number of available plans is expected to remain nearly flat=E2=
=80=945,532 versus 5,553=E2=80=94while more than 99% of beneficiaries retain =
access to at least one MA plan and 97% have at least 10 choices. CMS projects=
 34 million enrollees, or 47.4% of Medicare beneficiaries. (
CMS, =E2=80=9CMedicare Advantage and Medicare Prescription Drug Programs Expe=
cted to Remain Stable in 2027,=E2=80=9D September 28, 2026
). (
cms.gov
)
The national totals obscure substantial portfolio rotation. Analysis of the C=
MS files found heavy product reductions by UnitedHealthcare and Humana offset=
 partly by continued growth in special-needs plans. UnitedHealthcare separate=
ly confirmed that it is discontinuing plans and changing service areas effect=
ive January 1, with nonrenewal notices dated October 2. (
Healthcare Dive, =E2=80=9CHere=E2=80=99s how much insurers are cutting Medica=
re Advantage plans for 2027,=E2=80=9D September 29, 2026
;
UnitedHealthcare, =E2=80=9C2027 Medicare Advantage service area changes,=E2=
=80=9D October 1, 2026
). (
healthcaredive.com
)
Implication
The prior thesis is now more precise: MA margin repair is being pursued throu=
gh local product selection, network structure and benefit design rather than =
broad premium increases or a nationwide withdrawal. Average premiums therefor=
e overstate stability because they do not capture changes in cost sharing, su=
pplemental allowances, provider access or forced member switching.
The directional benefit for carriers is improved 2027 risk selection and redu=
ced exposure to structurally unprofitable markets. Providers face greater ref=
erral and contracting concentration as enrollment shifts toward narrower HMO =
and SNP products. Distribution, member-comparison and provider-directory plat=
forms should expect elevated demand during open enrollment, particularly wher=
e nonrenewals require beneficiaries to choose replacement coverage.
Watch next
Enrollment migration during the October 15=E2=80=93December 7 open-enrollment=
 period, especially whether product exits move members toward competitors or =
into carriers=E2=80=99 narrower successor plans.
2. Transparency in Coverage shifts from file production to governed data infr=
astructure
What happened
On October 5, the Departments of HHS, Labor and Treasury finalized revisions =
to the Transparency in Coverage rules. Plans and issuers will report one in-n=
etwork-rate file per provider network rather than per plan, move in-network a=
nd out-of-network files from monthly to quarterly publication, and exclude cl=
inically implausible provider-service combinations using the same taxonomy lo=
gic employed in claims adjudication.
The rules also add provider-taxonomy and utilization files, lower the out-of-=
network reporting threshold from 20 claims to 11, require standardized file-l=
ocation information and mandate attestations that the disclosures are complet=
e and accurate. Each file must identify the CEO, president or designated seni=
or official responsible for oversight. The departments estimate approximately=
 $174.5 million of annual net savings for plans and issuers beginning in the =
second implementation year. (
CMS, =E2=80=9CTransparency in Coverage Final Rules,=E2=80=9D October 5, 2026
). (
cms.gov
)
The in-network and out-of-network amendments apply five months after Federal =
Register publication; the new taxonomy, utilization and location files apply =
after 11 months. Telephone access to personalized cost-sharing information ap=
plies for plan years beginning January 1, 2027. Prescription-drug file develo=
pment begins in November 2026, with publication expected to start in December=
 2027. (
CMS, =E2=80=9CNew Regulations Make It Easier to Find, Compare, and Report Hea=
lthcare Pricing and Coverage Information,=E2=80=9D October 5, 2026
). (
cms.gov
)
Implication
The opportunity is moving away from brute-force hosting and ingestion of enor=
mous duplicate files. Competitive advantage will increasingly come from provi=
der identity resolution, taxonomy mapping, contractual-rate normalization, ut=
ilization context and defensible data-quality controls.
For plans, quarterly reporting and network-level files reduce infrastructure =
expense, but senior-official attestation turns data accuracy into an enterpri=
se compliance issue. For employers and providers, more usable out-of-network =
and utilization data should improve benchmarking and contract analysis. Vendo=
rs dependent primarily on storage volume face pressure; platforms that connec=
t transparency data to claims, network design and contracting gain relevance.
Watch next
Technical guidance specifying the single machine-readable format and how regu=
lators evaluate taxonomy exclusions, utilization completeness and executive a=
ttestations.
3. GLOBE survives as a mandatory model but loses most of its proposed economi=
c reach
What happened
CMS finalized the Global Benchmark for Efficient Drug Pricing Model, effectiv=
e November 30. The mandatory manufacturer model begins January 1, 2027; inter=
national-benchmark rebates and reduced beneficiary coinsurance start April 1 =
and continue through March 31, 2032, with reconciliation extending into 2034.=
 Selected geographic areas will represent approximately 25% of Original Medic=
are Part B beneficiaries. (
CMS Innovation Center, =E2=80=9CGLOBE Model,=E2=80=9D updated October 2, 2026
). (
cms.gov
)
The final model is materially narrower than proposed. CMS estimates that cove=
red utilization will represent less than 1% of non-dual, non-340B Original Me=
dicare Part B drug spending. Estimated annualized Part B savings fell from $2=
.3 billion in the proposal to $80 million in the final rule; cumulative estim=
ates include $298 million for Original Medicare, $288 million from subsequent=
 MA benchmark effects and $39 million for Medicaid. CMS estimates $177 millio=
n of beneficiary premium and coinsurance savings. The standard provider add-o=
n remains 6% of average sales price for most drugs. (
Federal Register, =E2=80=9CGlobal Benchmark for Efficient Drug Pricing Model,=
=E2=80=9D October 2, 2026
). (
govinfo.gov
)
Implication
GLOBE is not a broad reset of oncology or specialty-infusion economics. Its i=
mmediate burden is operational: selected providers must identify eligible ben=
eficiaries and drugs, collect the correct reduced coinsurance and reconcile g=
eographically variable claim treatment. Because Medicare adjusts its payment =
when beneficiary coinsurance falls, the model does not directly reduce the al=
lowed provider payment.
The longer-term payer effect is small but directionally negative for MA rebat=
es: lower Original Medicare spending flows into benchmarks beginning with 202=
8 rate setting. Technology demand should concentrate in eligibility, charge-c=
apture and patient-responsibility workflows rather than widespread drug-cost =
management.
Watch next
CMS=E2=80=99s selected geographic areas and initial drug list, which will det=
ermine which oncology practices, hospital outpatient departments and billing =
platforms must implement the model.
4. OIG expands the Medicare preclusion gap into ownership and pharmacy contro=
ls
What happened
Prior audits showed that suppliers removed from Original Medicare could conti=
nue billing MA plans. OIG now reports that MA organizations and Part D plans =
paid $72 million during 2022=E2=80=932024 for services associated with 249 of=
 1,017 revoked provider identifiers reviewed.
OIG attributed the payments to delays and errors in CMS=E2=80=99s Preclusion =
List, failure to list some organizations whose owners were excluded or convic=
ted of felonies, and regulations that do not subject dispensing-only pharmaci=
es to preclusion. OIG issued no formal recommendations but said the findings =
could support future regulation or guidance. (
HHS-OIG, =E2=80=9CMedicare Advantage Organizations and Prescription Drug Plan=
s Paid $72 Million During a 3-Year Period for Services Associated With Exclud=
ed Providers or Individuals Convicted of Certain Felonies,=E2=80=9D October 1=
, 2026
). (
oig.hhs.gov
)
Implication
NPI-level screening is insufficient when exclusion risk can sit with an owner=
, affiliated organization or dispensing pharmacy. Plans need entity-resolutio=
n controls that connect enrollment status, ownership, felony and exclusion da=
ta across Parts A, B, C and D.
The direct $72 million finding is modest relative to total managed-care spend=
ing, but the control failure creates recovery, audit and reputational exposur=
e. It strengthens demand for continuously refreshed provider-master data and =
prepayment screening rather than periodic list checks.
Watch next
Whether CMS expands the Preclusion List=E2=80=99s ownership and pharmacy scop=
e or places explicit screening obligations on MA and Part D sponsors.
What I'm Watching Next
October 6, 2026:
Scheduled Federal Register publication of the Transparency in Coverage final =
rules. (
Federal Register, public-inspection notice, October 5, 2026
). (
federalregister.gov
)
October 15, 2026:
Medicare open enrollment begins, providing the first market test of 2027 MA n=
onrenewals and product redesign.
October 21, 2026:
Comment period closes on preliminary 2027 Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule ra=
tes. (
CMS, September 21, 2026
). (
cms.gov
)
Bottom Line
The week=E2=80=99s most important change is the resolution of the 2027 MA lan=
dscape: national access remains broad, but carrier-level product pruning conf=
irms that margin repair is occurring through localized exits, narrower design=
s and member migration. MA plan continuity requires the closest attention. Op=
en enrollment beginning October 15 is the next event most likely to reveal wh=
ether retrenchment improves payer mix without materially weakening competitiv=
e position.
