s-cms-9882-f" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">CMS, =E2=80=
=9CTransparency in Coverage Final Rules,=E2=80=9D October 5, 2026</a>). (<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/transparency-coverage-final-r=
ules-cms-9882-f" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">cms.gov</=
a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The in-network and out-of-network amen=
dments apply five months after Federal Register publication; the new taxonomy=
, utilization and location files apply after 11 months. Telephone access to p=
ersonalized cost-sharing information applies for plan years beginning January=
 1, 2027. Prescription-drug file development begins in November 2026, with pu=
blication expected to start in December 2027. (<a href=3D"https://www.cms.gov=
/newsroom/press-releases/new-regulations-make-it-easier-find-compare-report-h=
ealthcare-pricing-coverage-information" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoratio=
n:underline;">CMS, =E2=80=9CNew Regulations Make It Easier to Find, Compare, =
and Report Healthcare Pricing and Coverage Information,=E2=80=9D October 5, 2=
026</a>). (<a href=3D"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/new-regulat=
ions-make-it-easier-find-compare-report-healthcare-pricing-coverage-informati=
on" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">cms.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The opportunity is moving away from br=
ute-force hosting and ingestion of enormous duplicate files. Competitive adva=
ntage will increasingly come from provider identity resolution, taxonomy mapp=
ing, contractual-rate normalization, utilization context and defensible data-=
quality controls.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">For plans, quarterly reporting and net=
work-level files reduce infrastructure expense, but senior-official attestati=
on turns data accuracy into an enterprise compliance issue. For employers and=
 providers, more usable out-of-network and utilization data should improve be=
nchmarking and contract analysis. Vendors dependent primarily on storage volu=
me face pressure; platforms that connect transparency data to claims, network=
 design and contracting gain relevance.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Technical guidance specifying the sing=
le machine-readable format and how regulators evaluate taxonomy exclusions, u=
tilization completeness and executive attestations.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-3" name=3D"story-3" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>3. GLOBE survives as a mandatory =
model but loses most of its proposed economic reach</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS finalized the Global Benchmark for=
 Efficient Drug Pricing Model, effective November 30. The mandatory manufactu=
rer model begins January 1, 2027; international-benchmark rebates and reduced=
 beneficiary coinsurance start April 1 and continue through March 31, 2032, w=
ith reconciliation extending into 2034. Selected geographic areas will repres=
ent approximately 25% of Original Medicare Part B beneficiaries. (<a href=3D"=
https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/globe" style=3D"c=
olor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">CMS Innovation Center, =E2=80=9CGLOB=
E Model,=E2=80=9D updated October 2, 2026</a>). (<a href=3D"https://www.cms.g=
ov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/globe?utm_source=3Dopenai" style=
=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">cms.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The final model is materially narrower=
 than proposed. CMS estimates that covered utilization will represent less th=
an 1% of non-dual, non-340B Original Medicare Part B drug spending. Estimated=
 annualized Part B savings fell from $2.3 billion in the proposal to $80 mill=
ion in the final rule; cumulative estimates include $298 million for Original=
 Medicare, $288 million from subsequent MA benchmark effects and $39 million =
for Medicaid. CMS estimates $177 million of beneficiary premium and coinsuran=
ce savings. The standard provider add-on remains 6% of average sales price fo=
r most drugs. (<a href=3D"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-02/h=
tml/2026-20281.htm" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">Federa=
l Register, =E2=80=9CGlobal Benchmark for Efficient Drug Pricing Model,=E2=80=
=9D October 2, 2026</a>). (<a href=3D"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-=
2026-10-02/html/2026-20281.htm" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underl=
ine;">govinfo.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">GLOBE is not a broad reset of oncology=
 or specialty-infusion economics. Its immediate burden is operational: select=
ed providers must identify eligible beneficiaries and drugs, collect the corr=
ect reduced coinsurance and reconcile geographically variable claim treatment=
. Because Medicare adjusts its payment when beneficiary coinsurance falls, th=
e model does not directly reduce the allowed provider payment.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The longer-term payer effect is small =
but directionally negative for MA rebates: lower Original Medicare spending f=
lows into benchmarks beginning with 2028 rate setting. Technology demand shou=
ld concentrate in eligibility, charge-capture and patient-responsibility work=
flows rather than widespread drug-cost management.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS=E2=80=99s selected geographic area=
s and initial drug list, which will determine which oncology practices, hospi=
tal outpatient departments and billing platforms must implement the model.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-4" name=3D"story-4" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>4. OIG expands the Medicare precl=
usion gap into ownership and pharmacy controls</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Prior audits showed that suppliers rem=
oved from Original Medicare could continue billing MA plans. OIG now reports =
that MA organizations and Part D plans paid $72 million during 2022=E2=80=932=
024 for services associated with 249 of 1,017 revoked provider identifiers re=
viewed.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">OIG attributed the payments to delays =
and errors in CMS=E2=80=99s Preclusion List, failure to list some organizatio=
ns whose owners were excluded or convicted of felonies, and regulations that =
do not subject dispensing-only pharmacies to preclusion. OIG issued no formal=
 recommendations but said the findings could support future regulation or gui=
dance. (<a href=3D"https://oig.hhs.gov/reports/all/2026/medicare-advantage-or=
ganizations-and-prescription-drug-plans-paid-72-million-during-a-3-year-perio=
d-for-services-associated-with-excluded-providers-or-individuals-convicted-of=
-certain-felonies/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">HHS-OI=
G, =E2=80=9CMedicare Advantage Organizations and Prescription Drug Plans Paid=
 $72 Million During a 3-Year Period for Services Associated With Excluded Pro=
viders or Individuals Convicted of Certain Felonies,=E2=80=9D October 1, 2026=
</a>). (<a href=3D"https://oig.hhs.gov/reports/all/2026/medicare-advantage-or=
ganizations-and-prescription-drug-plans-paid-72-million-during-a-3-year-perio=
d-for-services-associated-with-excluded-providers-or-individuals-convicted-of=
-certain-felonies/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">oig.hh=
s.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">NPI-level screening is insufficient wh=
en exclusion risk can sit with an owner, affiliated organization or dispensin=
g pharmacy. Plans need entity-resolution controls that connect enrollment sta=
tus, ownership, felony and exclusion data across Parts A, B, C and D.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The direct $72 million finding is mode=
st relative to total managed-care spending, but the control failure creates r=
ecovery, audit and reputational exposure. It strengthens demand for continuou=
sly refreshed provider-master data and prepayment screening rather than perio=
dic list checks.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Whether CMS expands the Preclusion Lis=
t=E2=80=99s ownership and pharmacy scope or places explicit screening obligat=
ions on MA and Part D sponsors.</p>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
